WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.060
MongoDB security in Node.js is the practice of making document access intentional, authorized, validated, least-privileged, observable, and recoverable.

2
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:19.640
Start by inventorying MongoDB access across APIs, workers, scripts, admin tools, exports, imports, and CI/CD.

3
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.420
Secure connections with authentication, TLS, protected connection strings, and reviewed driver options.

4
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:32.480
Make sure the application knows who it connects as, what that identity can do, and how connection failures will be handled safely.

5
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.240
Keep query structure under application control.

6
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.820
Do not pass request objects directly into filters or update documents.

7
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:47.440
Validate externally influenced values, constrain dynamic query features, and reject unsupported operators or shapes.

8
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.980
Use schemas, validation, and allowlisted update fields to control document shape.

9
00:00:53.080 --> 00:01:08.200
Enforce object-level and tenant-aware authorization server-side, return only appropriate fields, and avoid exposing sensitive data or internal metadata through responses, logs, exports, and support tools.

10
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:28.400
A practical first week improvement plan is manageable: search for direct request-object queries, review update allowlists, verify tenant filters, check connection string handling, review database user privileges, confirm TLS and network restrictions, reduce sensitive logging, and add tests for rejected query operators and unauthorized document access.

11
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:36.180
Then turn those checks into pull request expectations, CI/CD controls, monitoring, and incident response procedures.

12
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:43.000
The durable win is not a one-time cleanup; it is a safer pattern for every future MongoDB feature.
